mpc2000xl: the record region is circular, and the send field is three bits

Replaces the previous 0055 wholesale. Everything here was measured with an
automated rig (scripts drive the panel from inside the machine via ioport
and a known stereo probe - 440 Hz left, 660 Hz right - goes in while
:speaker is captured), not inferred from listening.

THE RECORD REGION WRAPS, IT DOES NOT STOP. The write ran unbounded at
first - pos0 reached 352828 against a 4416-word region, scribbling over
wave RAM and replacing the kit already under the pads. Stopping at the end
fixed that and broke something else: adc_words climbed to 220488 while
wave_words froze at 8832, exactly the two regions, so the machine kept
capturing and nothing more reached wave RAM. The monitor voice then played
one static ~100 ms region round and round - the repeating sound - and the
level meters flickered because nothing refreshed what they scan.

The ADC's DMA channel is auto-init, which means it wraps rather than
halting, so the region is a circular buffer continuously rewritten while
the monitor voice reads it. After this, adc_words and wave_words track
each other exactly (1278896 vs 1278907) instead of diverging.

THE SEND DESTINATION IS THREE BITS. channel_remap has eight entries and
was indexed with a four-bit field. The MPC2000XL programs 0x1a and 0x1e
among others, giving indices 10 and 14 - out of bounds, with the result
used as an output channel. The chip has eight discrete outputs; under a
three-bit reading the observed values land in range and stay distinct.

WORKAROUNDS REMOVED. warm_capture_input, the monitor mix into outputs 0/1,
suppressing the record transfer's key-on, and keying the record voices off
at transfer end are all gone. The key-on in particular was not stray: it
is the machine keying its own monitor voice, and suppressing it was
fighting correct behaviour.

WHAT IS STILL WRONG, precisely located. In STEREO mode both inputs reach
the DSP (INPUT ch0=0.0436 ch1=0.0193 with L=1 R=1) and only the right is
ever heard. The firmware programs voice 21 hard left (L=80 R=00) and voice
23 hard right (L=00 R=80) as the monitor pair, and keys on ONLY voice 23 -
measured across a full run, ch21 keyons 0, ch23 keyons 4. So the left
input is captured and has no voice to play it. How the hardware keys the
left half of that pair is the open question; note playback pairs are N and
N+16 while this pair is 21/23, so the pairing rule is not uniform.

Also unexplained: the ring occasionally drains (depth 30 dipping to 2-4),
which is the residual meter flicker.

The diagnostics that found all this are included, gated on
MAME_MPC_SAMPLE_DEBUG.

